{
  // Type-checking for the shadcn-vue compatibility trial.
  //
  //   cd packages/vue-native && bun x vue-tsc --noEmit -p ../../apps/shadcn/tsconfig.json
  //
  // Nothing here emits: `build.ts` transpiles the `.ts` with bun and the `.vue`
  // with @vue/compiler-sfc, so this file exists so that vue-tsc and an editor
  // agree with what actually ships.
  //
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // WHY THIS IS NOT apps/tsconfig.json
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // The other four demo apps share one program next door (apps/tsconfig.json),
  // and that program is deliberately NOT a browser: `lib` is ESNext alone and
  // "vue" is redirected to packages/vue-native/src/runtime.ts, so a name the
  // host does not ship fails to compile. That is the right contract for code
  // WRITTEN for vue-native.
  //
  // This app is the opposite experiment. Everything under src/components/ui is
  // shadcn-vue v2.2.0 fetched verbatim from upstream (tools/fetch-registry.ts),
  // it is PORTED BROWSER CODE, and the question it exists to answer is whether
  // browser code runs here unmodified. So it is type-checked as the browser code
  // it is, and the places where the host cannot deliver are runtime advisories —
  // which is what the compat-first policy asks for. Checking it against the
  // runtime's smaller surface instead would report a "failure" on every line
  // upstream wrote, and there would be nothing left to measure.
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ESNext",
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",

    // DOM, unlike every other tsconfig in this repo. Not an oversight and not a
    // convenience: `HTMLAttributes['class']` from "vue" is in the props of all
    // 73 upstream components, and reka-ui's, @vueuse/core's and
    // lucide-vue-next's own .d.ts files are written against `HTMLElement`,
    // `Element`, `PointerEvent` and `MouseEvent` throughout. Without lib.dom
    // this app does not have a type error, it has several thousand.
    //
    // What lib.dom asserts here is what the SOURCE believes, not what the host
    // provides. The runtime installs the subset reka-ui actually reaches for
    // (closest/matches/querySelector, getBoundingClientRect, getComputedStyle,
    // MutationObserver, DocumentFragment, the pointer events) and warns at
    // runtime for the rest — see packages/vue-native/src/runtime.ts.
    "lib": ["ESNext", "DOM", "DOM.Iterable"],

    "strict": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    // On for the usual reason and one extra: this app's node_modules is a
    // published-package graph (reka-ui alone is ~2.6 MB of .d.ts), and its
    // internal type errors are not ours to fix or to read.
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    // tailwindcss-animate and a few of the smaller deps are `export =` CJS.
    "esModuleInterop": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "forceConsistentCasingInFileNames": true,
    "noImplicitOverride": true,

    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      // shadcn's own convention. tools/fetch-registry.ts rewrites `@/lib/utils`
      // to a relative path on the way in, so nothing under src/ uses this
      // today; it is here so that a component pasted straight from the shadcn
      // docs resolves the way its author meant it to.
      "@/*": ["./src/*"],

      // ---- the "vue" decision ---------------------------------------------
      // "vue" is DELIBERATELY NOT MAPPED. It resolves the ordinary way, to this
      // app's own node_modules/vue (3.5.41) — the real published package —
      // rather than to packages/vue-native/src/runtime.ts, for three reasons:
      //
      //   1. `HTMLAttributes`, `ButtonHTMLAttributes` and the rest of the
      //      element-attribute types live in @vue/runtime-dom. runtime.ts
      //      re-exports @vue/runtime-core and NOTHING else, so a mapping would
      //      break `class?: HTMLAttributes['class']` in all 73 components at
      //      once — in code we are committed to keeping byte-identical.
      //   2. `paths` rewrites bare specifiers EVERYWHERE, node_modules
      //      included, and reka-ui's own .d.ts files import from "vue" too. The
      //      mapping would silently retarget a dependency's declarations at a
      //      module that does not have what they ask for.
      //   3. runtime.ts is written against lib=ESNext plus its own
      //      engine.d.ts, which declares `console`, `Event`, `document` and the
      //      timers as globals. Those collide head-on with lib.dom above, so
      //      the two cannot share a program even if 1 and 2 were solved. The
      //      runtime is type-checked by its OWN tsconfig, under the platform it
      //      really runs on; checking it a second time under a browser lib
      //      would be checking a lie.
      //
      // At RUNTIME "vue" is of course the runtime — build.ts aliases it and
      // src/loader.ts seeds it. The gap between that and what is typed here is
      // the compatibility surface this whole app exists to measure.
      //
      // "vue-native" is bound to the same real package for the same reasons: it
      // is the same module as "vue" at runtime (loader.ts seeds both names to
      // one module), and app code imports exactly one symbol from it. The one
      // signature where the two genuinely differ is asserted at its call site
      // in src/main.ts, where it is visible, rather than hidden in a mapping.
      "vue-native": ["./node_modules/vue"]
    },

    // No @types package may put an ambient global in scope. The only one
    // installed is @types/web-bluetooth, a @vueuse/core dependency that its own
    // .d.ts references directly, so nothing here needs auto-inclusion — and a
    // global that type-checks and does not exist at runtime is precisely the
    // bug class this conversion is meant to find.
    "types": []
  },

  // Template checking, minus the two checks this app cannot honestly pass.
  //
  // `checkUnknownProps` / `checkUnknownEvents` reject any attribute or listener
  // a component does not declare. shadcn components declare only their own
  // props and forward the rest through `$attrs` and reka-ui's `Primitive`, so
  // `<Button disabled>`, `<Button @click="…">`, `<Input placeholder="…">` and
  // every `id` in this app are legal, documented, upstream usage that those two
  // checks call errors. Turning them on would mean editing the trial's own
  // usage to satisfy the checker — the one thing it must not do.
  //
  // Everything else strictTemplates turns on is KEPT, and it earns its place:
  // it is what type-checks the `v-model` write-back direction, i.e. the values
  // a reka-ui primitive can put BACK into a ref. That is where the real
  // mismatches in this app were (a Select that can emit `null` into a `string`,
  // a Checkbox that can emit `"indeterminate"` into a `boolean`).
  "vueCompilerOptions": {
    "strictTemplates": true,
    "checkUnknownProps": false,
    "checkUnknownEvents": false
  },

  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.vue"]

  // OUT OF SCOPE, both deliberately:
  //
  //   tailwind.config.ts — a BUILD-TIME node module. jiti/bun loads it inside
  //     the Tailwind JIT, it reads `__dirname` and imports `node:path`, so
  //     checking it here would mean adding `@types/node` to `types` above and
  //     putting Node's globals in every component's scope. apps/tsconfig.json
  //     and packages/vue-native/tsconfig.json scope themselves the same way.
  //
  //   tools/*.ts — the same, more so: fetch-registry.ts and gen-theme.ts are
  //     bun scripts over `fs`, `path` and `fetch`. They run by hand, they emit
  //     nothing that ships, and this app installs no @types/node for them.
}
